In article <1992Nov30.112341.20613@yang.earlham.edu>
davidw@yang.earlham.edu writes:
>1.  I was doing pretty well with a 6th(?) level priest when I descended to 
>level 5 and found about 8 Modor Orcs waiting for me.  I managed to take a 
>few out with a ricocheting Wand of Magic Missle, but they got me anyway.  
>I've gotten down as far as level 8 (with a Tourist!:)) and I've never seen 
>anything like that before or since.  What happened?

Orcs, like some other monsters such as rats or jackals, tend to be
created in groups so that the game usually creates a platoon of them
rather than just one.  In that situation, go back upstairs to heal or
try to find some way to fight them one at a time.

>2.  I guess my clever cat/dog stole one too many things from one of the 
>shops, becuase it looked like the shop keeper killed it.  Does this happen 
>often?

Not unless you're wearing a ring of conflict, which causes other
monsters to attack each other.

>3.  I've found rings of Teleport and Polymorph, but they don't seem to do 
>anything when I wear them.  How can I tell if I'm polymorphed (other than 
>using a stethescope) and how do I teleport?

Rings of teleport and polymorph don't affect you the instant you put
them on.  When you're wearing them, there is a certain probability
that you will be teleported or polymorphed.  Unless you also have a
ring of teleport/polymorph control, you will have no control over
where you will be teleported or what you will be polymorphed into.

>4.  What is a Ring of Combat?

No such thing.  You must mean a ring of Conflict (see above).

